b6447b7a17e4c5fcfb192dcaf4c2bb1ea3a926961d25e94832972bee1.png)\n\n  \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $b,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e8/e8d223a9a8a29f297a88f7d7c98d99776540e0ba0e0f3935892a356e92a8aac9.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1efe561b-931b-53c4-9b0e-a643d610589c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$c$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513612} {:answer/id #uuid "42c84b07-2f62-52e9-bdc6-a52d496edd88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a$ and  $c$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513709} {:answer/id #uuid "65978adb-3323-5e91-b18a-85cb0e6dea28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513806} {:answer/id #uuid "bae334dc-9624-5afb-8deb-183c087c84b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$b$ and  $c$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514027} {:answer/id #uuid "fb2393fd-dbeb-5257-9716-4245e4b3e22d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$b$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$b$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514186} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4d40d9e-5b20-5652-9681-0c46dcea53cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514346}] :question/id #uuid "b3de56e3-5772-5207-831f-4a8a7dca824e" :question/math-academy-id "q-148488" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dc03c3e8b7769622b363924604f242d0cf7f80eab6e0019eba5a3e95d6bc3c10.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for a sample of paired observations is shown above. What vectors correspond to the direction of the second principal component of the data?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *first principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance).\n\nThe *second principal component* of the data corresponds to the direction perpendicular to the first principal component in which the data varies the most.\n\nIn our diagram, the direction where the variance changes the most corresponds (approximately) to the vector $a,$ as shown below. So, $a$ defines the direction of the first principal component. \n\n![](images/4f/4f751057ec2ba51c7132b75ed4e0309269288601ebf19b4f13de0a5b54e6c615.png)\n\n  \n\nNext, notice that in the 2D case, we have only one direction perpendicular to the first principal component. And this direction corresponds to the vector $b,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7c272bdfda7665b4892155335ead5cf79b08b98e19a04a6211f2a628a0161c28.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$b$ only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Second Principal Component From a Scatter Plot"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184346 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184047 :question/id #uuid "10f2c954-d70e-56eb-b981-ad393a620521" :question/math-academy-id "e-21886" :question/problem "A geologist has measured two specific features, $x_1$ and $x_2,$ for each rock among a sample of rocks she is studying. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular rock.\nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately \n![](images/5d/5d1c7f2402d1142e6d7ab3787a2459e19607a5aaf97761c3dff917a5a74deb42.svg)\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the geologist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/97/97a46ed0c33579b24a5c43003b908ad7b04c78a3e5b5486d4bdc5d0fe2bb3bee.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n![](images/a8/a8013014e445cf1099127a588812b42312c7ce7d307b23a21b9b496509949e5a.svg)\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables $Y = XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features $y_1$ and $y_2$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in $y_1$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation ![](images/88/888217508e2871244251b77c465094fa6e581ccc36b92aa1ebc62a98f8a25654.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.447 & -0.894 \\\\ 0.894 & -0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.447{x}_{1}+0.894{x}_{2} & -0.894{x}_{1}-0.447{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that $y_1 = -0.447 x_1 + 0.894 x_2$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\ny_1 = -0.447 x_1 + 0.894 x_2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2ed4a0-7a12-5b55-adfc-32f705af4e99" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c64a9d-85b7-54d4-85e2-b8629081ee3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.743{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513640} {:answer/id #uuid "528de0bd-c0e1-5533-b0aa-55c1f79b9edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.743{x}_{1}-0.669{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513761} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba53d25-2ed0-5535-8d49-9d22d605ebad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513857} {:answer/id #uuid "9d37f248-ad0b-5895-b853-5ba2f3d76a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.669({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513938} {:answer/id #uuid "c743983c-ef48-5d67-86bf-908e9cc1b4b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.669{x}_{1}-0.743{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513857} :answer-field/id #uuid "850a97c7-43d9-546d-8101-67fdfb947421" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183115}] :question/id #uuid "e9148622-4d91-5cc0-b3f6-f75f09b1b9e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343703" :question/problem "An engineer measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each road bridge in a city. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular bridge. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.669 \\\\ 0.743\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.743 \\\\ -0.669\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the engineer wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/c4/c47327219f03934832e62176db1441a94d9a78edda2f0d398a63b2942ec6a845.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.669 & 0.743 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.669\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.669 & 0.743 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.669\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2} & 0.743{x}_{1}-0.669{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.669{x}_{1}+0.743{x}_{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfa7464f-6eb2-54a7-b815-f3930d0c9781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.447x_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183122} {:answer/id #uuid "eaea4935-27ec-5ee4-8a8c-9e9ce478bed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.894x_{1} - 0.447x_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183123} {:answer/id #uuid "eee34766-b22b-560e-ab9d-8523c1f5d7c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.894(x_{1} + x_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183124} {:answer/id #uuid "f1646d1a-ee64-5142-a96b-e390bb8b17d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.447x_{1} + 0.894x_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183125} {:answer/id #uuid "6f149e28-6f4e-5025-a4eb-337e64087aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.894x_{1} + 0.447x_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183126}] :answer-field/id #uuid "685089ec-5d64-5743-bc2f-ed1402f1d5e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183121}] :question/id #uuid "ca4703b2-b31a-5344-af23-59c755d2b8df" :question/math-academy-id "q-344071" :question/problem "A scientist measured two particular features, $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$, for each athlete in a team. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular athlete.\nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n$v_{1} \\approx \\begin{bmatrix}0.894 \\\\ 0.447\\end{bmatrix}, v_{2} \\approx \\begin{bmatrix}-0.447 \\\\ 0.894\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the scientist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003afbd0-71df-5851-ac97-766aac2021f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.707{x}_{1}+0.707{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513526} {:answer/id #uuid "0323a014-9e20-5787-b0d1-3bef4eb4e870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513536} {:answer/id #uuid "4aded3ac-f955-5149-a241-69be81cf8871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.707({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513735} {:answer/id #uuid "59762be5-4665-590a-a8b0-f439efc3af1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.707{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513772} {:answer/id #uuid "8b043862-3c88-5ffc-9063-4108680f5b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513536} :answer-field/id #uuid "e66bcdbe-e3e0-5f78-be6e-bc00cded069c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514333}] :question/id #uuid "2381e2c6-90e5-5e9a-8022-1f6b70cb4895" :question/math-academy-id "q-344084" :question/problem "A neuroscientist measured two specific features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each brain image of a group of patients he is studying. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular brain image. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 \\\\ -0.707\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the neuroscientist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/70/70a37afaaad54e3f384ad446ab2cf57bc0f21ffa8f6986f01841d9f4041f531c.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ -0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ -0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2} & -0.707{x}_{1}+0.707{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=-0.707{x}_{1}-0.707{x}_{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c54fdba-b389-5b54-9720-959b5f18971d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.832{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513561} {:answer/id #uuid "0e524028-ca98-575f-a980-f0530848a15a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513565} {:answer/id #uuid "1e4d4fb4-8dff-5794-a3cc-51edd2d40060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.832{x}_{1}+0.555{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513610} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce71e51-1c0f-564c-a052-a0ab97df49d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.555{x}_{1}-0.832{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513690} {:answer/id #uuid "d2231053-6aed-5e86-8317-202410bd2a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.555({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513565} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cab87af-ca27-5d76-8fd5-6124194c1d37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514226}] :question/id #uuid "68cd1b00-9920-5784-9707-6f682f05d636" :question/math-academy-id "q-344087" :question/problem "A physicist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each electron released in an atomic experiment. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular electron. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.555 \\\\ 0.832\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.832 \\\\ 0.555\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the physicist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/17/171a9c86bf510c2ee5b65325d6f8939c65b5118286057934f91a053f75faf82f.png)\n\n  \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.555 & 0.832 \\\\ 0.832 & 0.555\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.555 & 0.832 \\\\ 0.832 & 0.555\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2} & 0.832{x}_{1}+0.555{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=-0.555{x}_{1}+0.832{x}_{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4401a834-4408-55a4-bed9-f955775055e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.890{x}_{1}-0.456{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513712} {:answer/id #uuid "9816a44e-fa5d-597c-a8ab-5c65208603f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.456({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513926} {:answer/id #uuid "cefe2cd0-91db-55d5-af14-8affe3de1778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.890{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514072} {:answer/id #uuid "d16e9923-2eb9-5f81-90f0-c065974959cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.456{x}_{1}-0.890{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514076} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fd7d42-929f-5eaf-a861-ae3e9d553b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514124} :answer-field/id #uuid "e68ffcc9-1fd2-5b21-96cd-20a43203877f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514334}] :question/id #uuid "7eb658cd-5383-5f28-8b76-9d708b2f6422" :question/math-academy-id "q-344079" :question/problem "A physicist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ for each oscillation produced when a mass hanging on a spring is displaced and then released. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular oscillation. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.456 \\\\ 0.890\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.890 \\\\ -0.456\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the physicist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/f0/f0f0623703d1a3767a7c3efd275e977c6933465faeb761991f00da705bac4a2c.png)\n\n \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.456 & 0.890 \\\\ 0.890 & -0.456\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.456 & 0.890 \\\\ 0.890 & -0.456\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2} & 0.890{x}_{1}-0.456{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.456{x}_{1}+0.890{x}_{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a26175-d1b6-5968-8d75-e79f81b8f4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.667({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513551} {:answer/id #uuid "241fe0b4-e84c-5b91-973c-3e2ccbadf541" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513633} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd3bf57-87be-5a0e-a26f-d05223e219cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.745{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513858} {:answer/id #uuid "a2558652-2910-5d66-aae1-99df04a43b38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.745{x}_{1}+0.667{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513951} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f09a11-3e11-526e-86f4-d3c7a493f6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.667{x}_{1}-0.745{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513633} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c4e8c66-d4aa-5c56-a392-1d26ce6b88cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514295}] :question/id #uuid "dd2cd7be-06ce-5ffb-8860-cd5d1eb5c130" :question/math-academy-id "q-344073" :question/problem "A meteorologist measured two particular features, ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ at various locations throughout California. The collected data was stored in a standardized observation matrix, where each row contains both features for a particular location. \nThe first and second principal components of the data are approximately\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}≈[\\begin{aligned}0.667 \\\\ 0.745\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.745 \\\\ 0.667\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nBy combining the two measured features linearly, the meteorologist wants to construct a single feature that preserves the maximum amount of variance of the collected data. Which of the following could be the best candidate for the new feature?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the direction in which the data varies the most (the direction where we have the maximum variance) corresponds to the *first principal component* of the data.\n\n![](images/f7/f7c823606bbbe77326819df5d487b5e0019dd7439b46124547f71681b9484f6d.png)\n\n \n\nLet  \n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.667 & -0.745 \\\\ 0.745 & 0.667\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nbe the matrix of principal components. \n\nThen, applying the change of variables $Y=XV,$ we can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are uncorrelated), and\n\n- the features ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance (meaning that most of the variance is concentrated in ${y}_{1}$).\n\nApplying our change of variables to a single observation $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]V \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.667 & -0.745 \\\\ 0.745 & 0.667\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2} & -0.745{x}_{1}+0.667{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that ${y}_{1}=0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}$ will contain most of the variance of the data. \n\nTherefore, the best candidate for the new single feature is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=0.667{x}_{1}+0.745{x}_{2}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing Principal Components as Linear Combinations"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3775 :topic/title "Introduction to Principal Component Analysis"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093069} :activity/id #uuid "d0d2bfed-fc08-50a9-93f7-83f4384c1054" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093438 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix ![](images/63/63dd03e425762fa8e9e2e0d33ce552245c6d1c00a12022c433329c3c747cdbba.svg) How can we find its eigenvalues?\n\nBy definition, we need to find the value(s) of $\\,\\lambda\\,$ such that $\\,A\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}\\,$ is true for some *non-zero* vector $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}.$ Let's re-write this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ Av-\\lambda v & =0 \\\\ Av-\\lambda Iv & =0 \\\\ (A-\\lambda I)v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation above tells us that there should be at least one non-zero vector $\\mathbf{\\color{blue}v}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Null}(A-\\lambda I)$, the null space of the matrix $A-\\lambda I.$ As a result, the invertible matrix theorem tells us that the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\,$ must be singular, meaning $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$\n\nWe've reduced the problem to finding the values of $\\lambda$ that satisfy the equation\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is called the **characteristic equation** of the matrix $A$. So, let's solve it!\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,-\\,\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\,-\\,\\lambda \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\,-\\,\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\,-\\,\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the characteristic equation $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-5\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ -8-2\\lambda +4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda=-3$ and $\\lambda=1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "992adb24-3547-5b71-bb96-561b2f90f721" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3559 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "aa3f5d68-5f04-578d-b46e-4900e8496c29" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092229 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbf5fbb2-688a-5833-87b9-37229c8fb692"} :step/id #uuid "89493310-e194-5bc4-bea0-893a5af03e16" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093072}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092230 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d235bca-d848-5075-affb-09e3103cfc11"} :step/id #uuid "04fc546d-cc73-521c-b37b-04a57e324df2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093074}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093439 :tutorial/content "The characteristic equation of any $2 \\!\\times\\! 2$ matrix $A$ is always a quadratic equation, which implies that $A$ has at most $2$ eigenvalues. Here are the all possible cases:\n\n- Two distinct real eigenvalues\n\n- One repeated real eigenvalue (or, equivalently, two real eigenvalues that are the same)\n\n- No real eigenvalues (or, equivalently, two complex eigenvalues)" :tutorial/id #uuid "3ec52280-5c5a-5e6f-bcbe-328634cbfecf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3560 :tutorial/title "The Number of Real Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "75bc9629-a0a2-557a-b062-e66b006ea327" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "864cf210-cbb0-5a79-b90a-33a5df332559"} :step/id #uuid "78e02a6a-545a-511e-a2f5-b45e357cb0fe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093071}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd4158d-6baf-5a61-96a2-74c66231d082"} :step/id #uuid "75d3f2ae-a3c8-5016-8ccf-895f6655ebd0"}] :activity/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092464}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050001 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092229 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092074 :question/id #uuid "aebe3048-cc5b-5340-a15e-d577ae2ee72a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5479" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n![](images/36/36c1ce5342657231cd78154ab4821a6a5bf9b78b74297ae5a4cdccdc2f6d5dcd.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I\\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/1c/1cab5be2ed7c560a3a5192473be4ff7b6cf6dc5728552f1d179c73affebefe20.svg)\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ 4-4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbf5fbb2-688a-5833-87b9-37229c8fb692" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e42bea0-ae31-5c53-973d-aa17ac450933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321378} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9bdffb-21f8-5d69-a0bd-17fe65627150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321615} {:answer/id #uuid "3f87dd55-fd67-54f3-a30e-392aed0248a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321646} {:answer/id #uuid "ac110cfb-d9fa-56d7-95de-4b61052b6737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322495} {:answer/id #uuid "f703d3fa-fa27-5c44-95ed-f194c20cdf12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323086} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d352f44-669a-5112-9b66-540b684a29de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323418}] :question/id #uuid "21a76447-bb7c-5d72-a771-38739632e899" :question/math-academy-id "q-36758" :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -8 & -6-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-(-8)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ -12-2\\lambda +6\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "938e10cf-d562-5150-9e82-fa7021ef8852" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ^{2} + 10λ - 25 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090341} {:answer/id #uuid "bc89e43c-c42e-5e1b-bf4e-5da50af307c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ^{2} + 25 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090342} {:answer/id #uuid "c731c860-6f51-5b6a-ae52-80c494949d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "λ^{2} - 10λ + 15 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090343} {:answer/id #uuid "f12f8c38-98